---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout title="Generative Editing — Varve Docs" description="Use Varve's local, mask-guided generative editing workflow for non-destructive Fill and Remove operations.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <article class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Tools / Generative Editing</nav>
      <h1>Generative Editing</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">A mask-guided, local-first workflow for reconstructing image regions while keeping the source layer and accepted result separate.</p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Open the tool</h2>
        <p>Select exactly one image layer and open <strong>Generative Edit</strong> from the image inspector's Adjustments section, Object → Generative Edit…, or the command palette. All three entry points open the same workflow. Mixed or multi-layer selections are rejected so the mask always has one unambiguous raster source.</p>
        <p>White means <strong>edit</strong>; black means <strong>preserve</strong>. Clear Paint resets the mask without changing the source image. Use Fit for an overview and 1:1 when checking fine edges.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Build and refine the mask</h2>
        <p>Paint directly over the source with the Brush control, or reuse an existing mask:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Use Pixel Selection</strong> imports the current document pixel selection.</li>
          <li><strong>Use Layer Mask</strong> imports the selected image's raster layer mask.</li>
          <li><strong>Invert</strong> swaps edit and preserve coverage. <strong>Show mask overlay</strong> lets you inspect the boundary before generating.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Expand mask grows the edit region to catch edge contamination. Feather softens the final composite edge. Context controls how much surrounding source imagery is supplied to the local reconstruction. These controls operate in source-image pixels and changing any of them clears the candidate, requiring a fresh preview.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Available modes</h2>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Availability</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Fill</td><td>Reconstruct painted pixels from nearby context.</td><td>Local, available</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Remove</td><td>Rebuild the background after painting an unwanted object.</td><td>Local, available</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Replace</td><td>Replace a region from a text instruction.</td><td>Staged; requires a verified prompt provider</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Expand</td><td>Synthesize pixels beyond the current image bounds.</td><td>Staged; requires a verified outpainting provider</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Quality and providers</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Fast</strong> uses the offline PatchMatch heuristic and needs no model download.</li>
          <li><strong>AI</strong> uses the optional LaMa inpainting model. Download it explicitly; the model is kept in local model storage.</li>
          <li>LaMa is image-conditioned, not text-conditioned. The prompt field is shown for the shared Fill/Replace/Expand workflow contract but is not used by the current local provider, and prompt text is not stored in the local edit record.</li>
          <li>There is no silent remote fallback. A future remote provider must state what it uploads and ask for consent immediately before transmission.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Review and accept</h2>
        <p>Use Original and Result to compare the source with the generated image. Fit, 1:1, zoom, and Center support both composition review and edge inspection. Regenerate creates a temporary variation entry; selecting a variation changes the candidate without writing document history.</p>
        <p>Apply inserts the accepted pixels as a sibling image layer in one undoable transaction and selects that new layer. The source remains intact. The document records the source relationship, mask asset, provider/runtime, quality, seed, accepted variation, and placement fingerprint. Rendering the accepted layer does not require the model to be installed.</p>
        <p>Undo and redo operate on the embedded scene data and do not run inference again. Save/reopen, Layers, the inspector, clipboard, and export therefore see the same accepted result.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section docs-note">
        <h2>When a result becomes stale</h2>
        <p>If the source image, placement, or document revision changes while a job is running, the result is discarded instead of being applied to the wrong pixels. Cancel also leaves the document unchanged. Generate again after making edits to the source.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>What this tool does not promise</h2>
        <p>PatchMatch and LaMa can produce useful reconstructions, but they do not understand semantic instructions, guarantee repeating patterns, or solve every transparent, reflective, or highly structured image. Inspect the result at 1:1 and keep the source layer until the edit is approved.</p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
  .breadcrumb { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: .875rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  h1 { color: var(--text-primary); font: 700 clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.8rem)/1.1 var(--font-editorial); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .docs-intro { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 3rem; line-height: 1.7; }
  .docs-section { margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  .docs-section h2 { border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-accent); color: var(--text-primary); font-size: 1.45rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; padding-bottom: .5rem; }
  .docs-section p, .docs-section li, td, th { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.8; }
  .docs-section li { margin-bottom: .5rem; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1.25rem 0; }
  th, td { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-default); padding: .75rem .5rem; text-align: left; vertical-align: top; }
  th { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: .8rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; }
  .docs-note { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-primary); padding-left: 1rem; }
  @media (max-width: 620px) { table { font-size: .9rem; } th, td { padding: .6rem .3rem; } }
</style>
